[Music]  in 2004 I set up an organization called  the London feminist Network and we  revived the women's reclaim the night  march they hadn't been won in London for  some time and they started in Britain in  the 1970s oh I'm going to go on and talk  about that history a bit more and we  revived the March and it started off  with 50 women in 2004 and it became  quite quite big over the years and went  on to we have you know over 2000 on  several occasions and those marches are  now held all across the UK as well so  I've been involved in feminist activism  for a long long time and also in policy  work around domestic violence prevention  and domestic violence prevention  education for local education  authorities worked with Women's Aid and  various other things and for my Phyllis  Phyllis book I interviewed and surveyed  over 100 fun feminist activists across  the UK some from the 1960s and 70s and  some activists who've only been involved  in the current resurgence so they were  all different generations of activists  they had a lot of different political  identifications the most popular that  people chose to label themselves when  they picked a label was socialist  feminist and socialist and feminist so  some people split them into two rather  than put them together and quite a lot  of radical feminists responded to my  survey I need to use as well which you  know methodologically is probably  because I know you know aligning myself  with that school of feminism's it  started in 1977 it's a women's march  against rape and all forms of male  violence against women and against  sexual harassment in the public sphere  it's not to be confused with the much  more recent [ __ ] walk which started in  Canada and the US that's a much more  recent invention reclaimed annoy is  about some of the same things but it  chooses to keep its clothes on which is  I think probably the most  or thing that women can do in this  culture is keep their clothes on and  open their mouths and say stuff instead  so [ __ ] walk of course got a lot of  media attention for men and women  wearing what would be considered you  know revealing or provocative clothing  whatever you want to call it I don't  even use that term Spartan I have lots  of issues with that much anyway perhaps  we can talk about some of that stuff  later so it began then well it began in  Leeds actually in the 1970s but there  was a lot of stuff leading up to it  really  so 71 Susan Brown Miller and the New  York radical feminists they held what is  the first documented speak out now speak  outs being quite popular and they're  much more well-known now this is where  survivors of broken male violence are  given a safe space to stand up and talk  about what what happened to them it was  seen at the time it's still seen now as  very taboo breaking it's seen as an act  of political act of breaking the silence  around sexual violence so then it was  considered even more to be breaking for  people to do this and then I suppose  really the history of reclaiming the  night as a social movement and as a  public protest march really lies in  Brussels so over International Women's  Day in March 1976 this tribunal was held  the International Tribunal on crimes  against women over 40 countries  represented over 2000 women attended it  was a test testimony to really about how  patriarchy works in all these different  countries about the form that male  violence against women and children took  in all these different countries you can  imagine it was hardly a heartening event  it must have been quite heartbreaking  actually listening to all those  different testimonies for the whole of  the conference so at the end of that  conference and goodbye all the things  that they'd heard the women decided to  hold a candle lit march through the  centre around where their conference had  been held and that is perhaps the first  sort of documenting nighttime women's  March industries again  violence against women and then the  women who'd been at that conference they  took news of it and off that protest  back to their own countries and to their  own women's groups where they were  organizing themselves and another  reclaim the night march was held in  Berlin it was after the rape and murder  of a young woman there and they  introduced the flaming torches and held  their nighttime marching in 77 and then  in April that year it caught on and  there was synchronised marches so these  are really the world's first  synchronised reclaim the night marches  they happened in several cities across  West Germany they were called Take Back  the Night then and they had slogans such  as women are not cattle to be looked  over women are not pieces of meat they  went out at midnight on the 30th of  April and this was reported in in  Courage magazine which was one of the  women separation magazines of Germany at  the time there was another more popular  one called EMA and which is actually  still running courage was the more sort  of DIY pamphleteer one and it was  reported in there along with photographs  as you can see and then thanks be to  women who could speak and read and write  several different languages we were  working at sparerib at the time when we  like a woman called Amanda Serb stn they  were there in charge of international  mules and it was Virge hog to scan the  women's liberation newsletters and  periodicals from from around Europe and  pick out sort of interesting news items  that might be good for spare rib Sperry  of course one of the you know the most  popular women's liberation periodical in  Britain so there it was it went in in  the August of the summer issue and it  covered it covered those marches in West  Germany and it's got a picture of them  there with their tape back then right  back but they call it reclaim night here  meanwhile in Leeds  a woman called al Garthwaite was  organizing in the Leeds revolutionary  feminist group at that time they'd all  been quite influenced by a paper that  Professor and academic course Sheila  Jeffries had given which was called  against the liberal takeover of our  movement and towards a revolutionary  feminism and she'd given that paper at  this women national women's liberation  conference was held at City Union King  Islington and several groups were very  inspired by that and they took they took  news of that back to their own groups  and in fact there was a specific  conference on revolutionary feminism  which was held in Edinburgh and another  activist Sandra McNiel who went on to be  very influential in British feminism and  particularly in policy work around  violence against children and the  roadman sexual abuse of children she was  at that conference as well and women  started saying has anyone seen the item  in sparerib about the big marches that  were held across Germany why don't we do  similar here and this was particularly  particularly resonated with women in  Leeds and I'm sure some of you will know  why the idea of women taking back the  streets and protesting against male  violence was particularly pertinent to  women organizing in the 1970s in Leeds  and Yorkshire it was of course because  of the acts of one of Britain's most  notorious serial killers Peter Sutcliffe  whom the press dubbed Yorkshire Ripper  now he murdered 13 women over this  period in the 70s and quite a lot of  them were sexual murders as well and so  feminist women were already organizing  around that in Leeds and indeed across  the north of England and so they they  were saying we need to do some similar  to what these women did in Germany we  should go out with flaming torches we  need to reclaim our towns and city  centres women already feel so unsafe  this is a sort of activism that we  should be organizing here and so the  march was advertised in wires which was  the women's information retrieval and  inquiry service which was a sort of  newsletter of the wings iteration  movement which had actually been set up  by our guard waiting the Leeds  revolutionary feminists they'd suggested  it at one of the national conferences I  think in 75 they said there's so much  going on now we need just a uk-wide  newspaper that has all the events and  groups as people are losing track of  everything that's going on that idea was  passed and Leeds published wires for a  number of years I think six or seven  years until it moved on to women in York  so you can see there they advertised it  women in West Germany demonstrated for  the right to walk unafraid and unhe salt  on the streets at night they were torch  lit processions throughout towns all  over West Germany women in Britain are  afraid to walk home at night especially  with all the recent rape reports fresh  in our minds couldn't we organize  similar national demonstration to help  tone our fear into anger and action and  that was the real motivation that you  can see coming out behind all the  reclaiming that marches when they  started

